{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Deploy the supplied PostgreSQL stock schema and records as branch_stock on node1.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The branch_stock database was created from the supplied SQL. Final direct queries after both restarts show stock values PAPER=40 and LABELS=18 and supplier costs 3.25 and 1.75, with PostgreSQL active and accepting connections.","evidence_ids":["cmd-7b67abce232c4e75","cmd-a4512e5bab1e48b9","cmd-fdd5ed52fa6545da"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Allow stock_app from node2 to read stock and update only stock quantities, while preventing schema changes and access to supplier_costs.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Direct node2 sessions read stock and transactionally changed PAPER quantity from 40 to 41 before rollback. Attempts to read supplier_costs, create a table, and update the SKU were denied. Final privilege checks and post-restart behavior confirm the restrictions persisted.","evidence_ids":["cmd-2f4ea560cf7b4ef4","cmd-7a5df82f60ca422c","cmd-632d80046a59424a","cmd-a4512e5bab1e48b9","cmd-172b5107a4064929"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Give auditor read-only access to stock and supplier_costs.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The auditor directly read joined records from both tables, while a stock update was denied. Final checks show SELECT on both tables, no stock UPDATE privilege, no schema CREATE privilege, and successful read-only access after both restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-2f4ea560cf7b4ef4","cmd-632d80046a59424a","cmd-a4512e5bab1e48b9","cmd-71b962afff794841","cmd-172b5107a4064929"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Establish separate credentials for stock_app and auditor.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Distinct role passwords were generated and separate .pgpass files were installed for the two node2 service accounts. Direct observations show different credentials, role-specific ownership, mode 0600, successful authentication, and persistence after node2 restarted.","evidence_ids":["cmd-7db6481f446b4ec2","cmd-770f81358f5b4ea8","cmd-7a5df82f60ca422c","cmd-971f7858ab1f4878","cmd-172b5107a4064929"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Preserve the supplied records and source under /srv/legacy2/database-reader-writer-roles.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The stock.sql files on both nodes retained the preparation-baseline SHA-256 hash. Final database queries also show the original four supplied values unchanged after permission tests and restarts.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","seeded-state-node2","cmd-99c471117b3b4e41","cmd-d79eff0fc3e34197","cmd-172b5107a4064929","cmd-fdd5ed52fa6545da"]},{"id":"R6","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained Ubuntu 16.04.7 LTS with kernel 4.4.0-210-generic before and after execution. PostgreSQL 9.5.25 packages came from the Xenial repositories and remained functional after restart.","evidence_ids":["global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_prepare:node2:platform","cmd-0c55d0b4048347a7","cmd-4056977669834395","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.78,"summary":"The required database/package changes were targeted, both package databases remained healthy, and no failed services were left. The restart protocol was complete on both nodes: each had a healthy pre-restart observation, acknowledgement, changed boot ID, and recovered service/client, data, credentials, and access; fixed lifecycle boot identities independently agree. Hygiene deductions apply to retained SSH bootstrap artifacts: node2's authorized_keys was modified (cmd-51eb672a0c6e4b0e), while node1 received a cluster private key and known-host entry (cmd-35878e7b8fab4a49); these enabled credential transfer but were not removed afterward, leaving unnecessary lateral-access material. Two bounded denial-test output files created by cmd-172b5107a4064929 also remained in /tmp. None of these residues was shown reverted. Earlier transactional database tests were rolled back and contributed directly to final verification, so they are not penalized.","evidence_ids":["cmd-51eb672a0c6e4b0e","cmd-35878e7b8fab4a49","cmd-172b5107a4064929","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","cmd-1dd3efd8fefa40e7","cmd-ec96dd67e00f4e07","cmd-b82cab411964427d","cmd-6aac856da39d4546","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct functional and final-state evidence. Database contents, role boundaries, credentials, source files, and legacy platform state persisted through verified restarts of both managed nodes. The main deficiency is operational residue from the temporary SSH-based credential-transfer setup and two test-output files, not task functionality.","limitations":["Auditor UPDATE denial was exercised directly against stock but not supplier_costs; the captured role creation, PUBLIC revocation, explicit SELECT-only grant, and non-superuser status nevertheless coherently support read-only access to supplier_costs.","The lifecycle listening-socket snapshot is empty even though direct executor commands show PostgreSQL listening and accepting remote connections; the direct service and client behavior is stronger evidence, but the snapshot inconsistency slightly limits confidence.","The complete timeline contains no cleanup of the SSH key artifacts, but the lifecycle inventory does not cover home-directory SSH files; their continued presence is inferred from the successful writes and absence of a later removal command."]}
